A = {'dogs/can-dogs-eat-grapes/': {'h1': 'Can dogs eat grapes?',
                               'topic': 'Food',
                               'img': 'dog-grapes',
                               'alt': 'A golden retriever puppy lying on the carpet at home',
                               'toxic': True,
                               'src': ['PPH_GRAPES', 'PPH_RAISINS', 'ASPCA_FOODS'],
                               'short': 'No. Grapes, raisins, sultanas and currants are toxic to dogs. Pet Poison Helpline says eating them can cause '
                                        'vomiting, belly pain or diarrhea, followed quickly by kidney injury and kidney failure. The ASPCA also lists grapes '
                                        'and raisins as foods to keep away from pets. Call a vet or a pet poison hotline if your dog eats any.',
                               'body': [['Pet Poison Helpline lists common grapes, muscadines, raisins, sultanas and Zante or Corinthian currants as toxic for '
                                         'dogs.',
                                         0],
                                        ['Poisoning starts with stomach and gut upset such as vomiting, belly pain and diarrhea, and is followed rapidly by '
                                         'kidney injury and kidney failure.',
                                         1],
                                        ['Tartaric acid in grapes and raisins is the suspected toxin, but this has not been proven. Most reported poisonings '
                                         'are in dogs.',
                                         1],
                                        ["Grapes and raisins also appear on the ASPCA's list of foods pets should not have, because of the risk to the "
                                         'kidneys.',
                                         2]],
                               'vet': ['Your dog ate any grapes, raisins, sultanas or currants',
                                       'Vomiting, diarrhea or belly pain',
                                       'Drinking or peeing much more or less than usual'],
                               'faq': [['What if my dog only ate one or two?',
                                        'Call anyway. Pet Poison Helpline notes that many factors, including the amount eaten and the size of the dog, decide '
                                        'what is toxic for a pet, so let an expert judge.'],
                                       ['Are cats at risk too?',
                                        'Pet Poison Helpline says poisoning is mainly reported in dogs, but it refers to companion animals in general, so keep '
                                        'grapes and raisins away from all pets.']]},
 'dogs/how-often-should-i-bathe-my-dog/': {'h1': 'How often should I bathe my dog?',
                                           'topic': 'Grooming',
                                           'img': 'dog-bath',
                                           'alt': 'A happy wet shepherd dog during a bath',
                                           'toxic': False,
                                           'src': ['ASPCA_DOG'],
                                           'short': 'Probably less than you expect. According to the ASPCA, a handful of baths each year is plenty for most '
                                                    'dogs, and regular brushing does much of the cleaning in between. Work out any tangles before the bath, '
                                                    'then rinse well, since soap left behind in the coat tends to collect dirt.',
                                           'body': [['For most dogs, a few baths a year is enough.', 0],
                                                    ['Brush often. It keeps the coat cleaner between baths and means less loose hair around the house.', 0],
                                                    ['Deal with mats first by combing or trimming them, then bathe, and finish with a thorough rinse so no '
                                                     'soap residue is left to attract dirt.',
                                                     0],
                                                    ['In warm months, look your dog over daily for fleas and ticks.', 0],
                                                    ['If your vet has prescribed a medicated shampoo or a skin treatment, follow that plan instead.', None]],
                                           'vet': ['Itching, redness, sores or hair loss',
                                                   'A strong smell from the skin or ears',
                                                   'Fleas or ticks you cannot get under control'],
                                           'faq': [['Does brushing replace bathing?',
                                                    'Not fully, but it helps a lot. The ASPCA suggests brushing often to keep the coat clean and to reduce '
                                                    'shedding.'],
                                                   ['What if my dog smells or itches a lot?',
                                                    'Ask your vet. Odor and itching can be signs of a skin or ear problem.']]},
 'dogs/can-dogs-eat-chocolate/': {'h1': 'Can dogs eat chocolate?',
                                  'topic': 'Food',
                                  'img': 'dog-chocolate',
                                  'alt': 'A white dog stretched out asleep on a white couch',
                                  'toxic': True,
                                  'src': ['ASPCA_FOODS', 'PPH_CHOC'],
                                  'short': 'No. Chocolate contains theobromine and caffeine, which can cause vomiting, diarrhea, panting, thirst, '
                                           'hyperactivity, an abnormal heart rhythm, tremors and seizures in dogs. Baking chocolate, cocoa powder and other '
                                           'dark chocolate are the most dangerous. If your dog eats chocolate, call your vet or a pet poison hotline right '
                                           'away.',
                                  'body': [['The risky compounds in chocolate are called methylxanthines, mainly theobromine and caffeine. Coffee and other '
                                            'caffeine products carry the same kind of risk.',
                                            0],
                                           ['Effects range from an upset stomach, restlessness and excess thirst to an irregular heartbeat, shaking and '
                                            'seizures. Large amounts can kill.',
                                            0],
                                           ['Dark and baking chocolate and cocoa powder are the most concentrated. White chocolate contains very little, but '
                                            'it is still not a treat for dogs.',
                                            0],
                                           ["How sick a dog gets depends on the amount and type of chocolate and the dog's size and health.", 1],
                                           ['Watch for less obvious sources such as cocoa mulch, hot cocoa, chocolate syrup, fudge and chocolate covered '
                                            'espresso beans.',
                                            1],
                                           ['Chocolate candy and baked goods can also contain other poisons, such as macadamia nuts, raisins or xylitol.', 1]],
                                  'vet': ['Your dog ate any chocolate, cocoa powder or chocolate baked goods',
                                          'Vomiting, diarrhea, panting or restlessness',
                                          'Tremors, seizures or a racing heart'],
                                  'faq': [['What if it was only milk or white chocolate?',
                                           "Call anyway. Risk depends on the type and amount and your dog's size. White chocolate has the least "
                                           'methylxanthine, but treats can hide other poisons.'],
                                          ['Is there a safe amount?',
                                           'Our sources do not give one for pet owners. Pet Poison Helpline asks you to call for a professional assessment.']]},
 'dogs/is-xylitol-toxic-to-dogs/': {'h1': 'Is xylitol toxic to dogs?',
                                    'topic': 'Food',
                                    'img': 'dog-xylitol',
                                    'alt': 'A fluffy brown dog sleeping on a kitchen floor',
                                    'toxic': True,
                                    'src': ['PPH_XYL', 'ASPCA_FOODS'],
                                    'short': 'Yes. Xylitol is a sugar free sweetener found in gum, mints, candy, baked goods, toothpaste and some vitamins. In '
                                             'dogs it can cause a dangerous drop in blood sugar, sometimes within 10 to 15 minutes, and liver failure. If your '
                                             'dog eats anything with xylitol, call your vet or a pet poison hotline right away.',
                                    'body': [['Xylitol is found in sugar free gum and mints, candy, puddings, sauces, syrups and jams, toothpaste, mouth '
                                              'rinses, chewable vitamins and other supplements, some nasal sprays and even makeup.',
                                              0],
                                             ['The amount varies widely between products. Even different flavors of one gum brand can contain different '
                                              'amounts.',
                                              0],
                                             ['If enough is eaten, xylitol can cause life threatening low blood sugar, sometimes within 10 to 15 minutes, and '
                                              'acute liver failure.',
                                              0],
                                             ['Early warning signs are throwing up, low energy and unsteady walking. Seizures may follow.', 1],
                                             ['Liver damage can happen within 12 to 24 hours and can lead to abnormal bleeding.', 1]],
                                    'vet': ['Your dog ate gum, mints, candy or any product that may contain xylitol',
                                            'Vomiting, tiredness or wobbly walking',
                                            'Seizures or collapse'],
                                    'faq': [['How fast does xylitol work?',
                                             'Fast. Pet Poison Helpline says blood sugar can drop within 10 to 15 minutes, so call as soon as you know.'],
                                            ['Can I use my own toothpaste on my dog?',
                                             'Some toothpastes contain xylitol, so use a pet toothpaste your vet recommends.']]},
 'dogs/how-often-should-i-feed-my-dog/': {'h1': 'How often should I feed my dog?',
                                          'topic': 'Food',
                                          'img': 'dog-feeding',
                                          'alt': 'A green spiral slow feeder dog bowl seen from above',
                                          'toxic': False,
                                          'src': ['ASPCA_DOG'],
                                          'short': 'That changes as your dog grows. Per the ASPCA, young puppies eat four times a day, then three by about '
                                                   'three months and two by about six months. Most adult dogs over a year old can eat once daily, though big '
                                                   'dogs and those at risk of bloat are better off with two smaller meals.',
                                          'body': [['Young puppies have small stomachs and high energy needs, so they eat often: four times a day at first, '
                                                    'dropping to three around three months and to two around six months.',
                                                    0],
                                                   ['After the first year, a single daily meal works for many dogs. Big dogs and breeds at risk of bloat '
                                                    'usually do better with the food split into two.',
                                                    0],
                                                   ['Treats and toppers such as cooked egg or vegetables should stay a small slice of the diet, roughly a '
                                                    'tenth at most.',
                                                    0],
                                                   ['Too many table scraps can unbalance nutrition, encourage fussy eating and add weight, so keep them rare.',
                                                    0],
                                                   ['Water should always be available. Clean the bowls regularly.', 0],
                                                   ['Ask your vet how much your dog should eat.', None]],
                                          'vet': ['Sudden weight loss or weight gain',
                                                  'Your dog stops eating or eats much less than usual',
                                                  'Vomiting or diarrhea after meals'],
                                          'faq': [['Why do big dogs get two meals?',
                                                   "Splitting food into two smaller meals is the ASPCA's suggestion for large dogs and dogs at risk of bloat."],
                                                  ['Can I add people food?',
                                                   'Only a little. The ASPCA suggests keeping extras to about a tenth of what your dog eats, since a lot of '
                                                   'table food can cause health and weight problems.']]},
 'dogs/can-dogs-eat-onions-or-garlic/': {'h1': 'Can dogs eat onions or garlic?',
                                         'topic': 'Food',
                                         'img': 'dog-grass',
                                         'alt': 'A Bernese Mountain Dog nibbling grass in a garden',
                                         'toxic': True,
                                         'src': ['ASPCA_FOODS', 'PPH_ONION', 'PPH_GARLIC'],
                                         'short': 'No. Onions, garlic, chives and leeks belong to the Allium family, which is poisonous to dogs. They can '
                                                  'upset the stomach and damage red blood cells, leading to anemia. Risk depends on the amount eaten, and '
                                                  'Japanese breeds such as Akitas and Shiba Inus are more sensitive. If your dog eats any, call your vet or a '
                                                  'pet poison hotline.',
                                         'body': [['Onion, garlic, chives and leeks are members of the Allium family. All are poisonous to dogs, cats and '
                                                   'livestock.',
                                                   1],
                                                  ['These plants can upset the digestive tract and harm red blood cells. Losing too many red blood cells is '
                                                   'called anemia.',
                                                   0],
                                                  ['Pet Poison Helpline lists nausea, vomiting and diarrhea among the effects of a poisonous dose.', 1],
                                                  ['Cats react to smaller amounts than dogs do. For dogs, the danger grows with how much they eat.', 0],
                                                  ['Some dog breeds are more sensitive, including Japanese breeds such as the Akita and Shiba Inu.', 2]],
                                         'vet': ['Your dog ate onion, garlic, chives or leeks, or food made with them',
                                                 'Vomiting or diarrhea',
                                                 'Your dog seems weak or unwell after eating them'],
                                         'faq': [['Is garlic as risky as onion?',
                                                  'Pet Poison Helpline lists garlic in the same Allium family with the same kind of red blood cell damage.'],
                                                 ['Are green onions and shallots included?',
                                                  'Yes. Pet Poison Helpline lists green onions and shallots among the alternate names for onion poisoning.']]},
 'dogs/can-dogs-eat-macadamia-nuts/': {'h1': 'Can dogs eat macadamia nuts?',
                                       'topic': 'Food',
                                       'img': 'dog-diarrhea',
                                       'alt': 'A pug sitting on a red patterned rug with a toy',
                                       'toxic': True,
                                       'src': ['ASPCA_FOODS', 'PPH_MACA'],
                                       'short': 'No. Macadamia nuts can leave dogs weak, shaky and unsteady, and can cause vomiting and a raised body '
                                                'temperature. The ASPCA says trouble tends to show up within half a day and usually passes in one to three '
                                                'days. The nuts are also very fatty, which can inflame the pancreas. Call your vet or a pet poison hotline.',
                                       'body': [['In dogs, these nuts can bring on weakness, wobbling, low mood, vomiting, trembling and overheating.', 0],
                                                ['Problems typically start within about half a day and most dogs are back to normal in one to three days.', 0],
                                                ['Pet Poison Helpline adds joint stiffness and being unable to walk because of weak back legs. How bad it gets '
                                                 'depends on the amount eaten.',
                                                 1],
                                                ['The cause of the poisoning is unknown, but it can affect nerves and muscles. The high oil content can also '
                                                 'cause pancreatitis.',
                                                 1],
                                                ['Fatty nuts in general, including almonds, pecans and walnuts, can upset the stomach and may trigger '
                                                 'pancreatitis.',
                                                 0]],
                                       'vet': ['Your dog ate macadamia nuts or cookies or candy containing them',
                                               'Weakness, wobbling, tremors or trouble walking',
                                               'Vomiting or feeling hot'],
                                       'faq': [['Will my dog be OK?',
                                                'Most dogs get better within a few days, according to the ASPCA, but call anyway so a vet can judge the amount '
                                                'eaten.'],
                                               ['What about chocolate covered macadamias?',
                                                'Call right away. Pet Poison Helpline warns chocolate treats can contain macadamia nuts, and chocolate is '
                                                'toxic too.']]},
 'dogs/is-ibuprofen-safe-for-dogs/': {'h1': 'Is ibuprofen safe for dogs?',
                                      'topic': 'Health',
                                      'img': 'dog-temperature',
                                      'alt': 'A black and white dog lying on an orange rug',
                                      'toxic': True,
                                      'src': ['PPH_IBU', 'ASPCA_DOG'],
                                      'short': 'No. Pet Poison Helpline says ibuprofen is a human pain reliever that should not be given to pets. In toxic '
                                               'amounts it can cause stomach ulcers with bloody vomiting or diarrhea, kidney damage and coma. Never give your '
                                               'dog medicine your vet did not prescribe, and call a vet or poison hotline if your dog swallows any.',
                                      'body': [['Ibuprofen is a non steroidal anti inflammatory drug, or NSAID, made for people. It should not be given to '
                                                'companion animals.',
                                                0],
                                               ['In toxic amounts it can cause stomach and gut ulcers, with vomiting and diarrhea that may contain blood.', 0],
                                               ['It can also harm the kidneys. Dogs may pee much more at first, then little or not at all.', 0],
                                               ['Severe cases can affect the brain and nervous system, causing depression, coma and death.', 0],
                                               ['Only give your dog medicine that a vet has prescribed for them. The ASPCA is clear on this.', 1]],
                                      'vet': ['Your dog chewed into a pill bottle or swallowed any ibuprofen',
                                              'Vomiting, diarrhea or blood in vomit or stool',
                                              'Peeing much more or much less than usual, or seeming dull or weak'],
                                      'faq': [['What can I give my dog for pain?',
                                               'Only what your vet prescribes. Human pain pills are a common cause of pet poisoning.'],
                                              ['My dog only ate one pill. Is that OK?',
                                               'Call anyway. Pet Poison Helpline says the amount eaten and the size of the animal decide what is toxic, so let '
                                               'an expert judge.']]},
 'dogs/is-marijuana-toxic-to-dogs/': {'h1': 'Is marijuana toxic to dogs?',
                                      'topic': 'Health',
                                      'img': 'dog-not-eating',
                                      'alt': 'Two dogs resting under a blanket on a bed',
                                      'toxic': True,
                                      'src': ['PPH_MJ'],
                                      'short': 'Yes. Pet Poison Helpline says there is no safe dose of psychoactive marijuana for pets. Eating it or breathing '
                                               'the smoke can cause stomach upset, stumbling, a dazed or aggressive state, sudden flinching and leaking urine. '
                                               'Severe cases can lead to collapse, seizures or trouble breathing. Call your vet or a pet poison hotline right '
                                               'away.',
                                      'body': [['Eating psychoactive marijuana can cause stomach and gut upset in pets.', 0],
                                               ['Nervous system signs include a stumbling walk, a dazed or aggressive mental state and sudden flinching. Pets '
                                                'may also leak urine.',
                                                0],
                                               ['Severe signs can include collapse, seizures and difficulty breathing.', 0],
                                               ['Breathing in marijuana smoke can also poison a pet.', 0],
                                               ['There is no safe dose of psychoactive marijuana for companion animals.', 0]],
                                      'vet': ['Your dog ate or was exposed to marijuana in any form',
                                              'Stumbling, a dazed look, flinching or leaking urine',
                                              'Collapse, seizures or trouble breathing'],
                                      'faq': [['Is secondhand smoke a risk?',
                                               'Yes. Pet Poison Helpline says inhaling psychoactive marijuana smoke can poison a pet.'],
                                              ['Is a small amount OK?',
                                               'No. Pet Poison Helpline says there is no safe dose of psychoactive marijuana for pets.']]},
 'dogs/can-dogs-eat-raw-bread-dough/': {'h1': 'Can dogs eat raw bread dough?',
                                        'topic': 'Food',
                                        'img': 'dog-limping',
                                        'alt': 'A person walking a black dog on a leash down a snowy street',
                                        'toxic': True,
                                        'src': ['ASPCA_FOODS', 'PPH_DOUGH'],
                                        'short': "No. Raw yeast dough keeps rising in a dog's warm stomach, which can cause painful bloating and, less often, "
                                                 'a twisted stomach that is life threatening. The yeast also makes alcohol, which can cause alcohol poisoning '
                                                 'with low blood sugar, weakness and seizures. If your dog eats raw dough, call your vet or a pet poison '
                                                 'hotline right away.',
                                        'body': [['Unbaked bread dough expands in the warm, moist stomach and can cause a bloated, swollen belly.', 1],
                                                 ['Less often, this can progress to a twisted stomach, called gastric dilatation volvulus or bloat, which is '
                                                  'life threatening.',
                                                  1],
                                                 ['Signs of bloat include vomiting, retching with nothing coming up, a swollen belly, a fast heart rate, '
                                                  'weakness and collapse.',
                                                  1],
                                                 ['As yeast ferments the dough, it makes alcohol, which is quickly absorbed. Alcohol can cause dangerous drops '
                                                  'in blood sugar, blood pressure and body temperature, and severe cases can lead to seizures.',
                                                  1],
                                                 ['Because alcohol gets into the blood so quickly, the ASPCA urges getting to a vet fast if a pet has '
                                                  'swallowed any.',
                                                  0]],
                                        'vet': ['Your dog ate any raw yeast dough',
                                                'A swollen belly or retching with nothing coming up',
                                                'Staggering, weakness, vomiting or seeming drunk'],
                                        'faq': [['Is baked bread OK?',
                                                 'Our sources warn about unbaked dough. The danger comes from the dough rising and the yeast making alcohol.'],
                                                ['Are cats at risk too?',
                                                 'Yes. Pet Poison Helpline says unbaked bread dough can be dangerous for both dogs and cats.']]},
 'cats/can-cats-drink-milk/': {'h1': 'Can cats drink milk?',
                               'topic': 'Food',
                               'img': 'cat-milk',
                               'alt': 'A tabby cat in a red holiday hat sitting by a little gnome figure',
                               'toxic': False,
                               'src': ['ASPCA_FOODS', 'ASPCA_CAT'],
                               'short': 'It is best to skip it. The ASPCA points out that pets have little of the enzyme needed to digest milk sugar, so milk '
                                        'and other dairy can lead to diarrhea or an upset stomach. Water is all a cat needs to drink. Check with your vet '
                                        'before adding any new food or drink.',
                               'body': [['Lactose is the sugar in milk. Pets have very little lactase, the enzyme that breaks it down.', 0],
                                        ['Undigested lactose can lead to loose stools and an upset stomach, and that goes for dairy foods as well as milk.', 0],
                                        ['Keep a clean bowl of fresh water out every day.', 1],
                                        ['Extras like treats should make up only a small part of what your cat eats, around a tenth or less.', 1]],
                               'vet': ['Diarrhea that lasts more than a day or two', 'Vomiting along with diarrhea', 'Your cat stops eating or seems tired'],
                               'faq': [['What about cheese or yogurt?',
                                        'They are dairy too, so the same stomach upset can happen. The ASPCA treats all dairy the same way.'],
                                       ['My cat drank some milk. Is that an emergency?',
                                        'Usually not, but watch for diarrhea or vomiting and call your vet if it continues.']]},
 'cats/why-is-my-cat-throwing-up/': {'h1': 'Why is my cat throwing up?',
                                     'topic': 'Health',
                                     'img': 'cat-throwing-up',
                                     'alt': 'A calm older tabby cat resting with eyes half closed',
                                     'toxic': False,
                                     'src': ['CORNELL_VOMIT'],
                                     'short': 'Occasional vomiting happens even in healthy cats, and Cornell notes that a hairball every week or two is not '
                                              'unusual. Call your vet promptly if it happens more than once a week, or if your cat also seems tired or weak, '
                                              'stops eating, vomits blood, drinks or pees more, or has diarrhea.',
                                     'body': [['An occasional bout of vomiting is often harmless.', 0],
                                              ['Hairballs are one of the usual reasons. Seeing one every week or two can be normal.', 0],
                                              ['Cats can also vomit after swallowing something they should not, such as toxic plants, spoiled or human food, '
                                               'medicines, or string and other small items.',
                                               0],
                                              ['Ongoing vomiting can point to an illness, from parasites and bowel problems to diseases of the kidneys, '
                                               'thyroid or other organs.',
                                               0],
                                              ['Expect your vet to ask questions, examine your cat and possibly run blood and stool tests or imaging.', 0]],
                                     'vet': ['Vomiting more than once a week',
                                             'Lethargy, weakness or poor appetite',
                                             'Blood in the vomit, more thirst, changes in peeing, or diarrhea at the same time'],
                                     'faq': [['Are hairballs normal?', 'Often, yes. Cornell considers one every week or two fairly common.'],
                                             ['Could my cat have swallowed something?',
                                              'It happens. Cats often swallow string and small household items. See your vet if you think so.']]},
 'cats/why-does-my-cat-have-diarrhea/': {'h1': 'Why does my cat have diarrhea?',
                                         'topic': 'Health',
                                         'img': 'cat-diarrhea',
                                         'alt': 'A calico kitten sitting in a green litter box',
                                         'toxic': False,
                                         'src': ['CORNELL_DIA'],
                                         'short': 'Often it passes quickly. Cornell notes that a sudden food change or a stressful event, like a long drive, '
                                                  'can bring on a few days of loose stool. It can also be a sign of infection, inflammation, organ or thyroid '
                                                  'disease, or cancer. See your vet soon if it lasts beyond a day or two and your cat seems unwell.',
                                         'body': [['Diarrhea is loose, watery stool that comes more often than usual and may smell strong or look pale.', 0],
                                                  ['Most cases are brief and settle on their own or after a diet adjustment.', 0],
                                                  ['Switching food suddenly or a stressful trip can set it off. Kittens moving onto solid food are more prone '
                                                   'to it.',
                                                   0],
                                                  ['When it keeps coming back, your vet may look for a longer term problem such as thyroid, kidney or liver '
                                                   'disease, an infection, or cancer.',
                                                   0],
                                                  ['Skip human diarrhea remedies, since Cornell cautions that some are unsafe for cats.', 0]],
                                         'vet': ['Diarrhea that lasts more than a day or two',
                                                 'Poor appetite, lethargy or vomiting along with diarrhea',
                                                 'Blood or mucus in the stool'],
                                         'faq': [['Can stress cause diarrhea in cats?',
                                                  'Yes. Cornell lists stress, like a long trip, as a cause of short episodes.'],
                                                 ['Can I give my cat a human diarrhea medicine?',
                                                  'No. Cornell cautions that some of these products can hurt cats. Ask your vet.']]},
 'cats/why-is-my-cat-not-eating/': {'h1': 'Why is my cat not eating?',
                                    'topic': 'Health',
                                    'img': 'cat-not-eating',
                                    'alt': 'An orange cat crouched beside a blue food bowl outdoors',
                                    'toxic': False,
                                    'src': ['CORNELL_HL', 'CORNELL_FEED', 'CORNELL_OFTEN'],
                                    'short': 'Do not wait it out. Cornell explains that when a cat stops eating, the body sends fat to the liver, which can '
                                             'lead to hepatic lipidosis, or fatty liver. Without prompt treatment it can be deadly. Overweight cats are at '
                                             'higher risk, and another illness is often the trigger. Call your vet if your cat refuses food.',
                                    'body': [['When a cat eats little or nothing, the body breaks down fat for energy and the liver can fill up with it. This '
                                              'is hepatic lipidosis.',
                                              0],
                                             ['Most cases start with another health problem that put the cat off food, for example diabetes, thyroid or kidney '
                                              'disease, pancreatitis or cancer.',
                                              0],
                                             ['Watch for hiding, skipping meals, drooling, or yellowish gums or ears.', 0],
                                             ['A cat that will not eat and is getting thinner needs a vet visit.', 1],
                                             ['Sore teeth can make crunchy food hard to eat. Ask your vet whether softer food could help.', 2]],
                                    'vet': ['Your cat refuses food or barely eats',
                                            'Weight loss, hiding or drooling',
                                            'Yellow tinge to the gums, skin or ears'],
                                    'faq': [['Why are overweight cats at higher risk?',
                                             'Any cat can get fatty liver, but Cornell says it shows up far more often in heavy cats.'],
                                            ['My cat is just picky. What can I do?',
                                             'Rotating a few different cat foods can help, but a cat that stops eating should still see a vet.']]},
 'cats/why-is-my-cat-so-itchy/': {'h1': 'Why is my cat so itchy?',
                                  'topic': 'Health',
                                  'img': 'cat-itchy',
                                  'alt': 'A tabby cat lying on fallen leaves beside a garden path',
                                  'toxic': False,
                                  'src': ['CORNELL_FLEAS'],
                                  'short': 'Fleas are a leading cause of itchy cats. Cornell explains that bites can be very itchy, and nonstop scratching can '
                                           'break the skin and let infection in. Cats allergic to fleas may develop small scabby bumps. Talk to your vet '
                                           'before using any flea product, since the wrong one or wrong use can hurt a cat.',
                                  'body': [['Fleas are small, dark insects, often no bigger than a pinhead.', 0],
                                           ['Their bites itch, and repeated scratching can leave sores that get infected.', 0],
                                           ['Look closely around the neck and near the tail, where fleas tend to bite. Allergic cats can get scabby bumps over '
                                            'larger areas.',
                                            0],
                                           ['Fleas can spread tapeworms and germs, and a heavy load can make kittens anemic.', 0],
                                           ['Clean the house as well as the cat. Vacuum carpets, rugs and furniture, or the fleas will return.', 0],
                                           ['Some flea products are risky for cats, so choose one with your vet.', 0]],
                                  'vet': ['Scratching that causes sores, scabs or hair loss',
                                          'Fleas on a kitten, or a kitten that seems weak or pale',
                                          'Itching that continues after flea control'],
                                  'faq': [['Can cat fleas affect people?', 'Yes. Cornell notes they can pass on germs that cause illness in people.'],
                                          ['Why treat the house too?', 'Fleas and eggs left in the home will reinfest your cat, per Cornell.']]},
 'cats/how-often-should-i-feed-my-cat/': {'h1': 'How often should I feed my cat?',
                                          'topic': 'Food',
                                          'img': 'cat-knead',
                                          'alt': 'An orange kitten stretching across a soft blanket',
                                          'toxic': False,
                                          'src': ['CORNELL_OFTEN', 'ASPCA_CAT'],
                                          'short': 'That depends on age. Cornell says kittens generally do best eating three times a day until about six '
                                                   'months, then twice a day until their first birthday. After that, one or two meals a day works for most '
                                                   'cats. Always leave fresh water out, and ask your vet how much your cat should eat.',
                                          'body': [['Growing kittens need a lot of food for their size, so smaller, more frequent meals help: around three a '
                                                    'day to six months, then two a day to one year.',
                                                    0],
                                                   ['Grown cats usually do fine on one or two meals daily, and older cats can stay on that schedule unless '
                                                    'your vet suggests a change.',
                                                    0],
                                                   ['Leaving dry food out suits some cats, but others eat too much. Swap out any leftover food daily.', 0],
                                                   ['Feed at times that suit your day, and stick to them.', 0],
                                                   ['Keep water fresh and available, and treat treats as a small extra, about a tenth of the diet or less.',
                                                    1]],
                                          'vet': ['Your cat refuses food or eats much less',
                                                  'Sudden weight loss or weight gain',
                                                  'Trouble chewing or dropping food'],
                                          'faq': [['Should I leave dry food out all day?',
                                                   'It can work for some cats, but not for those that overeat, according to Cornell. Refresh it daily.'],
                                                  ['Does my senior cat need a different schedule?',
                                                   'Usually not. Cornell says older cats can keep their routine unless your vet recommends otherwise.']]},
 'cats/is-tylenol-toxic-to-cats/': {'h1': 'Is Tylenol (acetaminophen) toxic to cats?',
                                    'topic': 'Health',
                                    'img': 'cat-temperature',
                                    'alt': 'A tabby cat dozing on a fluffy white blanket',
                                    'toxic': True,
                                    'src': ['PPH_ACET', 'ASPCA_CAT'],
                                    'short': 'Yes. Acetaminophen, the pain and fever reducer in Tylenol and many cold, flu and allergy products, is especially '
                                             'dangerous for cats, says Pet Poison Helpline. It can cause stomach upset, blood problems and liver failure. '
                                             'Never give your cat any medicine your vet did not prescribe, and call a vet or poison hotline if your cat '
                                             'swallows any.',
                                    'body': [['Acetaminophen is a common over the counter medicine that reduces pain and fever. It is also in some '
                                              'prescription pain medicines.',
                                              0],
                                             ['Check labels on products for cold and flu symptoms, allergies and fever, which may contain acetaminophen.', 0],
                                             ['It is safe for people, has a narrower margin of safety in dogs, and is especially dangerous for cats.', 0],
                                             ['Signs can include stomach and gut upset, blood problems and liver failure.', 0],
                                             ['Only give your cat medicine that a vet has prescribed for them. The ASPCA is clear on this.', 1]],
                                    'vet': ['Your cat swallowed any acetaminophen or a cold, flu or allergy medicine',
                                            'Vomiting or drooling',
                                            'Your cat seems weak, tired or unwell'],
                                    'faq': [['Is Tylenol safe for my dog instead?',
                                             'Ask your vet. Pet Poison Helpline says acetaminophen has a narrower margin of safety in dogs than in people.'],
                                            ['Which products contain acetaminophen?',
                                             'Pet Poison Helpline says to look for it in products labeled for cold and flu, allergies and fever.']]},
 'cats/are-lilies-poisonous-to-cats/': {'h1': 'Are lilies poisonous to cats?',
                                        'topic': 'Health',
                                        'img': 'cat-lilies',
                                        'alt': 'White Easter lily flowers in bloom',
                                        'toxic': True,
                                        'src': ['PPH_LILY', 'ASPCA_LILY', 'CORNELL_HAZ'],
                                        'short': 'Yes. Lilies from the Lilium genus, such as Easter, tiger and stargazer lilies, are highly toxic to cats. Any '
                                                 'amount of the leaf, stem, flower, pollen or even the vase water can cause kidney failure. If your cat '
                                                 'touches or chews a lily, take your cat to a vet immediately.',
                                        'body': [['All parts of lilies in the Lilium genus are toxic to cats, including any amount of leaf, stem, flower, '
                                                  'pollen, vase water or root.',
                                                  0],
                                                 ['Eating them can lead to vomiting, belly pain, dehydration and acute kidney failure. Early treatment is '
                                                  'vital after any exposure.',
                                                  0],
                                                 ["Many popular garden and bouquet lilies are on Pet Poison Helpline's danger list, including Easter and tiger "
                                                  'lilies.',
                                                  0],
                                                 ['The ASPCA also lists true lilies as toxic to cats, mainly because of kidney damage.', 1],
                                                 ["Cornell warns that even a tiny exposure can damage a cat's kidneys badly, so the safest option is not to "
                                                  'have lilies at home.',
                                                  2]],
                                        'vet': ['Your cat chewed, licked or touched any part of a lily, or drank vase water',
                                                'Vomiting, belly pain or not eating',
                                                'Drinking or peeing much more or less than usual'],
                                        'faq': [['My cat only licked some pollen. Is that a problem?',
                                                 'Yes. Pet Poison Helpline says any amount of pollen can be toxic. Go to a vet right away.'],
                                                ['Are lilies dangerous to dogs too?',
                                                 'The ASPCA does not list true lilies as toxic to dogs. The kidney danger applies to cats.']]},
 'cats/can-cats-eat-onions-or-garlic/': {'h1': 'Can cats eat onions or garlic?',
                                         'topic': 'Food',
                                         'img': 'cat-onions',
                                         'alt': 'A calico cat sitting on a dark rug looking up',
                                         'toxic': True,
                                         'src': ['ASPCA_FOODS', 'PPH_ONION', 'PPH_GARLIC'],
                                         'short': 'No. Onions, garlic, chives and leeks belong to the Allium family, which is poisonous to cats. Cats are more '
                                                  'sensitive than dogs. These foods can upset the stomach and damage red blood cells, which can lead to '
                                                  'anemia. If your cat eats any, call your vet or a pet poison hotline.',
                                         'body': [['Onion, garlic, chives and leeks belong to the Allium family, and all are poisonous to dogs, cats and '
                                                   'livestock.',
                                                   1],
                                                  ['These plants can upset the digestive tract and harm red blood cells. Losing too many red blood cells is '
                                                   'called anemia.',
                                                   0],
                                                  ['Cats react to smaller amounts than dogs do. For dogs, the danger grows with how much they eat.', 0],
                                                  ['Pet Poison Helpline lists nausea, vomiting and diarrhea among the effects of a poisonous dose.', 1],
                                                  ['Garlic is in the same family and carries the same risk.', 2]],
                                         'vet': ['Your cat ate onion, garlic, chives or leeks, or food made with them',
                                                 'Vomiting or diarrhea',
                                                 'Your cat seems unwell in any way after eating them'],
                                         'faq': [['Is a little garlic in food OK for cats?',
                                                  'Our sources say Alliums are poisonous to cats and cats are more sensitive than dogs. Ask your vet or a '
                                                  'poison hotline about any amount.'],
                                                 ['Are cooked or powdered onions safer?',
                                                  'Our sources list onion, garlic, chives and leeks as toxic without exceptions for cooking. Call your vet.']]},
 'cats/can-cats-eat-chocolate/': {'h1': 'Can cats eat chocolate?',
                                  'topic': 'Food',
                                  'img': 'cat-chocolate',
                                  'alt': 'A small tabby kitten staring at the camera',
                                  'toxic': True,
                                  'src': ['CORNELL_HAZ', 'ASPCA_FOODS', 'PPH_CHOC'],
                                  'short': 'No. Cornell puts chocolate on its list of human foods cats should not eat. It contains methylxanthines, which can '
                                           'cause vomiting, diarrhea, restlessness, an irregular heartbeat, shaking and seizures in pets. The darker the '
                                           'chocolate, the bigger the risk. If your cat eats chocolate, call your vet or a pet poison hotline.',
                                  'body': [['Cornell names chocolate among several human foods to keep from cats, alongside things like onions, grapes and '
                                            'xylitol.',
                                            0],
                                           ['The problem compounds are methylxanthines, also found in coffee and caffeine. They can upset the stomach, cause '
                                            'restlessness and thirst, and in bad cases affect the heart and cause seizures or death.',
                                            1],
                                           ['Dark and baking chocolate and cocoa are the most concentrated. White chocolate has very little.', 1],
                                           ["How sick a pet gets depends on the amount and type of chocolate and the pet's size and health. Chocolate treats "
                                            'can also contain other poisons such as raisins or xylitol.',
                                            2]],
                                  'vet': ['Your cat ate chocolate, cocoa powder or chocolate baked goods',
                                          'Vomiting, diarrhea, restlessness or panting',
                                          'Tremors, seizures or a racing heart'],
                                  'faq': [['Is white chocolate safe for cats?',
                                           "No. It has much less of the problem compound, but chocolate is still on the ASPCA's avoid list, and sweets can "
                                           'hold other harmful ingredients.'],
                                          ['What about coffee or tea?',
                                           'Caffeine is in the same chemical family as the compounds in chocolate, so the ASPCA flags it too.']]}}
